    <!-- Add Activity -->
     <axiom component="lesson-plan">
      <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
      <dl>
        <li>
          <title>Suggested Time</title>
          <p>15 minutes</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Objective</title>
          <p>To discover that the product of two square roots is the square root of their product.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Teacher's Role</title>
          <p>Provide pairs of square roots (e.g., <m>\sqrt{4}</m> and <m>\sqrt{9}</m>). Ask learners to find the product of the roots first, then find the root of the product (<m>\sqrt{4 \times 9}</m>). Challenge them to see if this works for non-perfect squares like <m>\sqrt{2}</m> and <m>\sqrt{3}</m> using calculators.</p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </axiom>

    <activity>
      <li>
                <title>Group Structure</title>
                <p>Learners work in collaborative groups of 3 to 4.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Materials Needed</title>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><p>Standard scientific calculators.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Group discussion notebooks or chart papers.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Pens and markers.</p></li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </li>
      <statement>
            <p>
                In your groups of 3 to 4, discuss, investigate, and record your findings for the following tasks:
            </p>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Investigate the relationship between multiplication and radicals:
                        <ol label="a">
                            <li><p>Calculate <m>\sqrt{4} \times \sqrt{9}</m> by finding the individual square roots first.</p></li>
                            <li><p>Now, calculate <m>\sqrt{4 \times 9}</m> by multiplying the numbers under the radical sign first.</p></li>
                            <li><p>Compare your answers from parts (a) and (b). Are they the same?</p></li>
                        </ol>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Using a scientific calculator, expand your investigation:
                        <ol label="a">
                            <li><p>Find the value of <m>\sqrt{2} \times \sqrt{8}</m>.</p></li>
                            <li><p>What is the exact value of the square root of 16 (<m>\sqrt{16}</m>)?</p></li>
                            <li><p>Based on your observations, what does this tell you about the relationship between <m>\sqrt{2} \times \sqrt{8}</m> and <m>\sqrt{16}</m>? Formulate a general rule in your group.</p></li>
                        </ol>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Apply your group's discovered pattern to division:
                        <ol label="a">
                            <li><p>Predict the exact result of <md>\frac{\sqrt{50}}{\sqrt{2}}</md> without using a calculator. Write down your group's collective reasoning.</p></li>
                            <li><p>Now, use your calculator to check your prediction. Did your group's rule hold true for division?</p></li>
                        </ol>
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
    </activity>



