    <!-- Add insight / key takeaway directly related to activity / learner experience -->

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
      <title>Structured Instruction</title>
      <p>
        <dl>
          <li>
            <title>Suggested Time</title>
            <p>10 minutes</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Objective</title>
            <p>To formalize the product and quotient rules for surds.</p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>
      <paragraphs>
        <title>Teacher Connection</title>
            <p>
             Emphasize that while we cannot add different radicands, we <em>can</em> multiply and divide them. Explain that <m>\sqrt{a} \times \sqrt{b} = \sqrt{ab}</m>.
            </p>
      </paragraphs>
    </axiom>

    <insight>
      <p>
        Unlike addition, multiplication and division of irrational numbers allow us to combine different radicands under a single radical sign.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Multiplication Rule: <m>\sqrt{a} \times \sqrt{b} = \sqrt{a \times b}</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        The Division Rule: <m>\frac{\sqrt{a}}{\sqrt{b}} = \sqrt{\frac{a}{b}}</m> (where <m>b \neq 0</m>)
      </p>
    </insight>
   <exploration>
    <title>Multiplying and Dividing Irrational Numbers</title>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-multiplying-dividing-radicals" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%" aspect="1:1" source="jsxgraph/multiplying-and-dividing-.js">
        <slate xml:id="jxgbox-mult-div" surface="jsxboard" aspect="1:1"/>
        <instructions>
            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Set the operation to multiplication (<m>\times</m>). Adjust the values of the components. Notice how the area of the green rectangle updates. How do the external coefficients affect the simplified radical's value compared to the numbers inside the root?
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Toggle the operation to division (<m>\div</m>). Observe the step-by-step arithmetic shown on screen. How does dividing the coefficients separately from the radicands mimic operations you have performed with algebraic variables?
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Can you find a combination of numbers where multiplying two irrational numbers creates a perfect integer answer? (Hint: Explore what happens when radicands <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> are identical).
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </instructions>
    </interactive>
</exploration> <!-- Add further instruction (not in a block) to develop ideas as needed -->

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <!-- Add at least 3 examples -->

     <example>
      <statement>Simplify: <m>\sqrt{3} \times \sqrt{12}</m></statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
            Apply the multiplication rule to bring both numbers under a single radical sign, then evaluate the resulting perfect square:
            <md>
                <mrow>\sqrt{3} \times \sqrt{12} \amp = \sqrt{3 \times 12}</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp = \sqrt{36}</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp = 6</mrow>
            </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
      <statement>Simplify: <m>2\sqrt{5} \times 3\sqrt{2}</m></statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
            Multiply the whole-number coefficients together, and separately multiply the components under the radical signs together using the multiplication rule:
            <md>
                <mrow>2\sqrt{5} \times 3\sqrt{2} \amp = (2 \times 3) \times \sqrt{5 \times 2}</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp = 6\sqrt{10}</mrow>
            </md>
            Since 10 has no perfect square factors other than 1 (<m>10 = 2 \times 5</m>), the surd is in its simplest form.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
      <statement>Simplify: <m>\frac{\sqrt{75}}{\sqrt{3}}</m></statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
            Apply the division rule to merge the two independent radicals into a single radical expression containing a fraction, simplify the fraction, and solve:
            <md>
                <mrow>\frac{\sqrt{75}}{\sqrt{3}} \amp = \sqrt{\frac{75}{3}}</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp = \sqrt{25}</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp = 5</mrow>
            </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
      <statement>Simplify: <m>\sqrt{2}(3 + \sqrt{2})</m></statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
            Use the distributive law to multiply the radical term outside the parentheses across each individual term inside:
            <md>
                <mrow>\sqrt{2}(3 + \sqrt{2}) \amp = (\sqrt{2} \times 3) + (\sqrt{2} \times \sqrt{2})</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp = 3\sqrt{2} + \sqrt{2 \times 2}</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp = 3\sqrt{2} + \sqrt{4}</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp = 3\sqrt{2} + 2</mrow>
            </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
      <statement>A rectangle has a length of <m>4\sqrt{6}</m> and a width of <m>\sqrt{2}</m>. Calculate its area.</statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
            The area of a rectangle is found by multiplying its length by its width:
            <md>
                <mrow>\text{Area} \amp = 4\sqrt{6} \times \sqrt{2}</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp = 4\sqrt{6 \times 2}</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp = 4\sqrt{12}</mrow>
            </md>
            Now, simplify the resulting surd <m>\sqrt{12}</m> by extracting its highest perfect square factor, which is 4:
            <md>
                <mrow>4\sqrt{12} \amp = 4\sqrt{4 \times 3}</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp = 4 \times \sqrt{4} \times \sqrt{3}</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp = 4 \times 2 \times \sqrt{3}</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp = 8\sqrt{3}\text{ square units}</mrow>
            </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
            An eco-conscious student group is constructing a cylindrical rainwater harvesting tank for their school garden. The exact radius of the circular base is designed to be <m>\sqrt{14}</m> meters. Using the formula for the area of a circle base <m>A = \pi r^2</m>, find the exact base area of the tank in terms of <m>\pi</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
            Substitute the given irrational radius <m>r = \sqrt{14}</m> into the circular area formula and apply the rule of multiplying a surd by itself:
            <md>
                <mrow>A \amp = \pi \times (\sqrt{14})^2</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp = \pi \times (\sqrt{14} \times \sqrt{14})</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp = \pi \times \sqrt{14 \times 14}</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp = \pi \times \sqrt{196}</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp = 14\pi\text{ m}^2</mrow>
            </md>
            Multiplying a square root by itself clears the radical sign completely, yielding the rational value 14.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
            A rural community school installs an off-grid solar power array. The total combined rectangular footprint of the panel layout has an area of <m>\sqrt{180}</m> square meters. If the narrow width of the installation framework is exactly <m>\sqrt{5}</m> meters, calculate its precise length.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
            The length of a rectangle is calculated by dividing its total area by its width:
            <md>\text{Length} = \frac{\text{Area}}{\text{Width}} = \frac{\sqrt{180}}{\sqrt{5}}</md>
            Apply the division rule to place both values under a single radical sign, then divide and simplify the remaining value:
            <md>
                <mrow>\text{Length} \amp = \sqrt{\frac{180}{5}}</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp = \sqrt{36}</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp = 6\text{ meters}</mrow>
            </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

